QSM Direct standalone profile for stock Proxmox VE 9
===================================================

This profile uses the official Proxmox packages. It adds a guarded PVE web
UI overlay and a separate HTTPS signalling service. It shares the native
preview's media worker, encoders and adaptive bitrate controller. Do not
install the patched qemu-server/pve-manager/pve-container/guest-common debs
from the native preview to use this release.

Install on the PVE node (Debian 13 / amd64):

    apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_0.4.0+stock1_amd64.deb \
                ./qsm-pve-direct-standalone_0.4.0+stock1_all.deb
    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --show

First installation chooses an available IPv4 port in 18007..18106, starting
at 18007, and saves it in /etc/qsm-pve-direct/signal.json. Port 8007 is never
selected. Existing saved configuration is preserved across upgrades.
The service and UI use the same configuration; reload the PVE page after
changing it. The port preflight does not displace another listener.

Set a local address and port explicitly:

    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --listen 192.0.2.10 --port 18009

Use 0.0.0.0 for all IPv4 interfaces, or :: for a dual-stack IPv6 listener.
By default the browser connects to the PVE page's hostname on that port.
When the browser needs a different address, including a NAT mapping:

    qsm-pve-direct-endpoint --listen 0.0.0.0 --port 18009 \
        --public-url https://pve.example.org:28009

The advertised address must route to this service and be covered by the
node's TLS certificate. --public-url auto returns to the PVE page hostname.
Changing an occupied bind address/port fails before modifying configuration.
The command reconciles the UI and restarts signalling; a failure restores
the previous configuration. Existing consoles may need reconnecting.

Allow the selected TCP port from operator networks. The service mirrors
pveproxy's node/custom certificate; a self-signed certificate may need a
browser exception at the signalling address too. WebRTC media still needs
UDP connectivity between the browser and node. NAT/TURN configuration is
separate. This profile does not transparently proxy other cluster nodes:
open the PVE UI of the node that runs the guest.

VM setup
--------
Reload PVE, stop the selected VM, then open Hardware -> Display -> Advanced
and enable QSM Display1. Choose a CPU display or the optional VirGL profile.
Start the VM and open Console. Add the QSM Console audio device for sound
and microphone. The CPU display retains noVNC; the GL display cannot offer
VNC/SPICE alongside QEMU's GL D-Bus display. Changing display configuration
requires a stop/start, not just an in-guest reboot.

QSM owns its private per-VM D-Bus in this profile; stock qemu-server does not
provide dbus-display or native webrtcproxy/webrtcsettings routes. Do not run
qsm-pve-direct-migrate: that command targets the patched native platform.
Existing native-preview VMs need a separately planned display conversion;
this package does not downgrade Proxmox or rewrite running VM configs.

LXC
---
Install the matching optional helper on the node:

    apt install ./qsm-pve-direct-lxc_0.4.0+stock1_all.deb
    qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable VMID --session wayland

The helper bundles qsm-console-guest and expects a Debian/Ubuntu container
with a Plasma desktop. See the main package README for GPU/session options.
The LXC Console menu offers QSM Direct; the terminal console remains.
Native SDDM and a local cursor require the same optional SDDM/Sway packages
and kernel capabilities as the native preview; they are not installed by
this profile. The host helper supports the stock pve-container dependency.

Updates/removal
---------------
The JavaScript include uses a reversible, version/hash-guarded dpkg-divert
of index.html.tpl. An unreviewed PVE UI update restores the stock template
instead of attempting a speculative patch. qsm-pve-direct-ui status reports
whether the overlay is active. Removing qsm-pve-direct restores the original
template. Configuration is retained; removing only the standalone profile
stops/disables its signalling service. Disable Display1 on stopped VMs
before removing the core package, so future starts have their normal display.

This remains a preview. The HEVC NVENC adaptive static-text sensitivity to
rare packet loss remains open; the media controller is unchanged here.
